"""Masses for the MuJoCo simulation export (Motion tab), user decision 2026-10-10.

Every link's mass and centre of mass equal microduck_rl's simulation/mass/body_mass_record.json
(sha256 28bd9ebedda311cf27e2ea934bb5fdfcf302a54e9b257135b466ba56270b9119),
frozen from feature/mouth-joint b939c76d: the model the robot's policies were trained on and
that the robot stands with (the sim-to-real reference). Inertia is
within 0.31 % of the record on the legs and trunk and 1.5 to 4.3 % on the head links. Generated by
microduck_rl simulation/mass/steve/write_mass_record_py.py; change the record
first, then regenerate this file. microduck_rl keeps the record as the source of its model.

- Head links (neck_pitch, yaw_roll_link_r05, head_frame_r05 and the jaw) are scaled by
  0.8507085670278253 so the head weighs the weighed 500 g; the grams below include it.
- DESIGN_MASS: printed parts whose record row came from their own closed mesh, and the two
  ankle bearing inner caps (aluminium; their record mass is the earlier steel estimate).
  design.mass overrides material x volume. centre: the instance's own frame, mm.
- LUMPED_MASS: every other record row (bought parts, fasteners, cables, the 15 servos, the
  battery pack) as a uniform box along `on`'s own axes; at: `on`'s own frame, mm.
- The 15 STS3215 cases (servo_*): their linked part has no material, so their solids add no
  mass; each servo's mass is a LUMPED_MASS row.

Known discrepancies kept from the record: the jaw servo row (jaw_servo) is a mass that predates the
STS3215 the robot fits; the bus servo adapter's 16 g sits on yaw_roll_link_r05 (the record's
link), while the board is on the head roll link; the caps' mass is the steel estimate.
"""

